Encoder experiments, November 2026
==================================

This file collects the measurements made on the slz encoder (src/slz.c)
after commit 7bafca4 ("WIP: readme update with new level"), on top of the
new level 2 (literal skip with a probe of the last word of each skipped
64-byte chunk) and the skipped indexing at level 3 (stride 256). They were
first reported in commit messages and in discussions; they are gathered
here so that they don't have to be run again.

Patches in this directory (originally commits 56734e4, 3836469 and
3c45512 on branch 20261108-lit-skip-2, rebased onto the v1.4 release
candidate 78686e9 where they apply in sequence). Their code is disabled
by default and absent from the preprocessed output when disabled (gcc -E
output identical to the base's):

  0001  slz: add SLZ_DRY_RUN and SLZ_STATS to measure the encoder
  0002  slz: rework FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH as a hash chain with a depth limit
  0003  slz: add FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH_MIN to try 3-byte matches

../brotli/0001 and ../lz4/0001 extend SLZ_STATS and apply on top of
these, in that order.

Corpora:
  - silesia.tar: 211947520 bytes (recoverable from tests/silesia.tslz with
    zdec, see the encoder benchmark notes);
  - hap-org.html: 932561 bytes, concatenation of 7 haproxy.org pages;
  - tests/index.html: 76799 bytes.

zenc options: -1..-4 = level, -T = text tree (at level 3 internally),
-k = block size in kB (4096 = default, 32 = haproxy-like buffers),
-l = loops, -t = no output, -v = report totout/ratio/MB/s.

Unless stated otherwise, timings come from a Xeon W-2145 (Skylake-SP),
gcc-15 -O3 -g -fomit-frame-pointer -march=native -mno-avx512f
-Wa,-mbranches-within-32B-boundaries -Wa,-malign-branch=jcc+fused+jmp+call,
taskset -c 2, zenc -l 3 -t -v, best of 3 runs, MB/s as printed by zenc.
They were NOT measured on the ARL (the reference platform) and should be
confirmed there before taking any decision based on them. Sizes do not
depend on the machine.

Note added later the same day: zenc used to release each 2 MB of the
mapped input with madvise(MADV_DONTNEED) once compressed, so every pass
faulted the file in again, measured ones included. That cost ~4% of each
pass (silesia -4 -k 32: 404-406 MB/s without, 421-422 with the new -p
option that pre-heats the input and keeps it mapped). All zenc speeds in
this file and in ../brotli/README / ../lz4/README were measured without -p: absolute
figures are ~4% low, comparisons between configurations are unaffected.
Use -p from now on. -p also pre-faults the output buffer (commit
a4183c6, merged as 449bc6e), which is calloc()'d at twice the block size
and was faulted in during the first pass (~22k faults at -k 262144).

A single pass (-l 1) remains ~10% slower than the following ones even
with -p, and this is not zenc: with the schedutil cpufreq governor, the
core's clock ramps up during the first pass (perf stat, silesia -p -1:
3.69 GHz on average for -l 1 vs 4.39 GHz for -l 2, same IPC 1.82/1.85,
same page faults). So either measure with -l 2 or more (the first pass is
never timed when looping), or set the governor to performance; anything
busy before the pass (e.g. pre-faulting a large output buffer) changes
the -l 1 figure by warming the clock up.


1. Measurement aids
-------------------

SLZ_DRY_RUN (bit field): the parts of the output that are not encoded,
while every decision is still taken as usual and the block headers are
still emitted. The output is not decodable.
  1 = literals sent in huffman blocks (fixed or text tree)
  2 = references (length + distance codes)
  4 = literals sent in stored blocks (the memcpy only)

SLZ_STATS: counters dumped on stderr at exit by a destructor: lookups,
word hits, hits within the window, references (count, lengths, distances,
encoded sizes, adjacency, backward extension), literal runs per block type
(huffman/stored), huffman blocks opened, literal-skip steps and probes,
and histograms of lengths, distances and run lengths. Not thread-safe,
accumulates over all calls: use zenc -l 1. The compressed output is
unchanged (checked at -1/-4, -k 32/4096).

FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH=N: replaces the bucket lookup with a walk along a hash
chain built over the whole block on entry. Among the first N earlier
positions carrying the same word (exact compare, 0 = all of them) within
the window, it keeps the longest match, the nearest one on equal lengths.
No match found means literal. Important: the chain has no collisions AND
indexes every position, including those covered by matches which the
hash table never sees (it only gets the looked-up positions plus 3 per
match via SLZ_INSERT_SKIPPED). So depth 1 is NOT "the current hash minus
collisions", it is that plus full indexing, and depth N is an upper bound
for N entries per bucket, not what such buckets would give. Parsing stays
greedy. All of silesia takes 3.9s at depth 0 (the old implementation
scanned the window byte by byte and was unusable on silesia; it also
stopped at the second matching candidate and equals depth 2 exactly).

FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH_MIN=3 (default 4): with FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH, keys the
chain on 3 bytes and takes 3-byte matches only when their code (7 bits of
length + 5..18 bits of distance in the fixed tree) is shorter than their
three literals (8 or 9 bits each). For ASCII this is a pure distance cut:
3-byte matches up to distance 8192. Dropped with the text tree, which has
no code for length 3.

Reproduce:
  make zenc USR_CFLAGS="-DSLZ_STATS=1";     ./zenc -4 -t silesia.tar
  make zenc USR_CFLAGS="-DSLZ_DRY_RUN=7";   ./zenc -4 -l 3 -t -v silesia.tar
  make zenc USR_CFLAGS="-DSLZ_STATS=1 -DFIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH=0 -DFIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH_MIN=3"


2. What the encoding format costs (SLZ_DRY_RUN)
-----------------------------------------------

silesia.tar, MB/s. d1 = no huffman literals, d2 = no references, d4 = no
stored copies, d3 = d1+d2, d7 = nothing encoded. Right columns: share of
the time spent encoding, i.e. 1 - def/dN.

  -k    lvl    def     d1     d2     d4     d3     d7  |  lits   refs    all
  4096  1    580.5  652.2  616.4  587.8  682.6  692.7  | 11.0%   5.8%  16.2%
  4096  2    515.8  580.0  544.3  518.6  608.9  624.5  | 11.1%   5.2%  17.4%
  4096  3    489.2  554.0  515.9  489.8  585.8  582.9  | 11.7%   5.2%  16.1%
  4096  4    469.7  532.3  495.9  470.3  561.2  563.6  | 11.8%   5.3%  16.7%
  4096  T    439.3  482.1  474.1  439.4  537.8  545.7  |  8.9%   7.3%  19.5%
  32    1    609.4  688.5  640.2  609.5  722.7  725.6  | 11.5%   4.8%  16.0%
  32    2    523.7  593.3  552.0  528.5  627.5  639.5  | 11.7%   5.1%  18.1%
  32    3    479.1  549.2  505.0  479.0  576.0  574.1  | 12.8%   5.1%  16.5%
  32    4    457.2  521.0  483.3  459.6  553.6  557.8  | 12.2%   5.4%  18.0%
  32    T    419.4  461.2  449.9  417.5  507.2  514.1  |  9.1%   6.8%  18.4%

Findings:
  - the format costs 16-19% of the time; 81-84% is the match search and
    the decisions. That's where the big gains would be;
  - huffman literals are two thirds of the encoding cost although they are
    only ~15% of the bytes: at -4 -k 4096, ~1.7 ns per literal (31.1M of
    them) vs ~1.3 ns per whole reference (18.7M, 9 bytes each on average);
  - stored copies are free (d4 within noise of def);
  - d1 and d2 are roughly additive (d3).

Leads derived from this (not tried yet):
  - batch several literal codes into a single enqueue56() (six 9-bit codes
    fit in 56 bits), but huffman runs average only 4.3 bytes, 39% of them
    a single byte, so it would need a short fast path for 2-3 literals;
  - the remaining per-lookup cost is roughly 6 ns at -4, dominated by the
    bucket hit test mispredict (65% of all mispredicts, measured earlier).


3. Statistics on silesia (SLZ_STATS)
------------------------------------

Per level. lookups = words looked up in % of input bytes; hits = same word
found in the bucket, % of lookups; inwin = hits within the window, % of
hits; taken = references emitted, % of hits in window; cover = % of input
covered by references; len/dist/bits = average length, distance and
encoded size of references; adj = % of references right after another
one (no literal in between); back = % extended backwards; hrun = average
huffman literal run (bytes); hbits = bits per huffman literal; stored =
bytes sent in stored blocks; skip = % of input passed over by the literal
skip.

