tests/index.html, ratio:

            hash   depth 1  depth 2  depth 4  depth 16   all
  -1      44.42%   43.06%   41.37%   40.13%   39.21%   38.96%
  -4      44.19%   43.00%   41.31%   40.07%   39.15%   38.90%
  -1 -T   37.37%   36.48%   35.37%   34.58%   34.08%   34.01%

(The old window-scan implementation gave 41.37% / 35.37%, i.e. depth 2.)

Findings:
  - collisions plus partial indexing cost 2.6% at 4 MB and 1.3% at 32 kB;
    this experiment can't tell which of the two dominates. They mostly
    lose matches (3.2 and 1.6 points more of the input left as literals);
  - the rest of the gap to the longest match (9.3% and 7.1%) comes from
    looking at a single candidate. Past depth 1 the literal share barely
    moves: matches get longer (9.05 -> 10.87) and twice as far
    (4142 -> 8568), so each costs 1.6 bits more but there are 13% fewer;
  - each doubling of the distance costs one more extra bit of distance
    code in fixed huffman, so searching further spends bits. It still pays
    per covered byte (2.268 bits/byte for hash, 2.383 at depth 1, 2.248 at
    depth 2, 2.162 at 4, 2.074 at 16, 2.035 for all) but the returns fall
    fast: 3.49 points for 1->2, 2.24 for 2->4, ~1.2 per doubling for
    4->16, 1.19 for 16->all;
  - depth 2 recovers about half of the total gap. This does not contradict
    the earlier rejection of 2 entries per bucket, which was decided on
    CPU cost (and real buckets would do worse than depth 2, see the
    caveat in section 1);
  - the chain keeps the longest match, not the cheapest in bits: a much
    farther match one byte longer may cost more. Ranking candidates by
    bits saved (8 * len - length code bits - distance code bits) instead
    was proposed, not tried.


5. 3-byte matches (FIND_OPTIMAL_MATCH_MIN=3)
--------------------------------------------

Idea: HTML has many short repeats at short distances ("</", "=\"", "<a "),
and a 3-byte match at short distance is cheaper than 3 literals in the
fixed tree (7 + 5..11 bits vs 24 bits up to distance 8192). The text tree
was left out since it has no code for length 3 (it would need to be
regenerated with tools/mktext.c, taking code space from other symbols).

-4, len3 = share of references of length 3 (-3 gives the same picture):

