  silesia  32    fom2-m3    93837879   -2.85%  25809481  39.49%   7.04  2151.4  19.50  14.24
  silesia  32    fom4-m3    90601962   -6.20%  24248819  35.39%   7.51  2486.6  19.78  14.11
  silesia  32    fom16-m3   87250015   -9.67%  22501559  31.36%   8.11  3189.0  20.28  13.91
  silesia  32    fom0-m3    85765657  -11.21%  21655567  30.33%   8.44  3799.0  20.63  13.78

(silesia m4 rows: see section 4.)

Findings:
  - keying on 3 bytes hurts at low depth (+8.2% on the HTML at depth 1):
    the most recent occurrence of 3 bytes is often only a 3-byte match,
    which shadows a longer one further back, and greedy parsing takes it;
  - with all candidates examined, 3-byte keys save only 0.15% (4 MB) and
    0.60% (32 kB) of the HTML's output over 4-byte keys, vs 1.43% and
    3.11% on silesia. On the HTML they do move 2.3 points of the input
    from literals to references (6.66% -> 4.36% at 4 MB), but the average
    match shrinks from 13.89 to 12.52 bytes with 14% more references: a
    short match taken greedily cuts into a longer one starting a few
    bytes later. It would need lazy matching on top of a deep search;
  - conclusion: not worth it with a few candidates per bucket and greedy
    parsing, and HTML is not where it would help.

An earlier experiment (commits 0fbac20 / 857c7ea on another branch,
SLZ_MIN_MATCH / SLZ_HASH_BYTES) had already found, with the real single-
entry hash: hashing on 3 bytes alone costs 4-5% of output (more
collisions, worse candidates for long matches), accepting 3-byte matches
another 1.7-5%, and hash-4/match-3 is byte-identical to hash-4/match-4.


6. Ideas discussed but not run, with the reasoning
--------------------------------------------------

Larger hash table (e.g. SLZ_HASH_BITS=15): the chain experiments above
don't depend on it (the chain filters on the exact word). Left aside
without measurement. Note that the table is on the stack and reset per
call: 15 bits = 256 kB to clear for each 16-32 kB haproxy buffer, and
far beyond L1.

Dropping text-mode references beyond 8192 or 16384 early to save CPU:
rejected on reasoning. Share of references beyond 8192 / 16384:
hap-org.html 17.6% / 7.5% at 4 MB, 10.0% / 2.8% at 32 kB; silesia 17.2% /
7.4% and 9.9% / 2.5%. A dropped reference (~11 bytes on the HTML) costs
~22 bits as a reference and ~75 bits as text literals, i.e. ~50 bits lost
each: in the order of 10-30% more output (minus what nearer partial
matches recover). And it would cost CPU, not save it: the lookup and the
word compare are already paid when the distance is known, the window test
is already a distance compare, and the bytes go through the literal path
instead (one lookup and one huffman literal each, the most expensive part
per byte, see section 2). The only CPU effect of far distances is the
cache footprint of text_dist_table (128 kB, far distances touch its last
96 kB); if it ever shows in a profile, computing the code for far
distances only is the way, not dropping the match (computing it for all
distances from the fixed one was measured 3-5% slower earlier).

A second candidate per bucket for level 4: level 4 is kept as the upper
bound with no compromise (large backward match, no literal skip). Two
candidates per bucket were measured long ago as costly in CPU for little
gain.


7. ARL results reported by Willy in the same period
------------------------------------------------------

Core Ultra 7 265K, Willy's own build, MB/s / bytes, silesia.tar.

Baseline at 7bafca4:
              4 MB                     32 kB
  l1   941.2  296350698       1011.7  323448042
  l2   828.0  276981102        868.3  295883856
  l3   795.5  274524147        800.9  290058198
  l4   746.5  274081503        750.6  289766550
  T    742.4  264743031        726.7  285421239

(zenc -l 3 here, hence totals 3x the single-pass sizes.)

Backward match reach 2 at level 2, i.e. SLZ_BACKWARD_MATCH { 0,1,2,2,8 }
instead of { 0,1,1,2,8 }:
  4 MB:  l2 822.4 MB/s, 276557460 bytes  (-0.7% speed, -0.15% size)
  32 kB: l2 860.1 MB/s, 295553982 bytes  (-0.9% speed, -0.11% size)
Rejected: 0.44% (4 MB) to 0.85% (32 kB) of speed per 0.1% of size, while
moving from level 2 to level 3 costs 0.40-0.44% per 0.1%.

Level 3 with skipped indexing at stride 256 vs level 4, 32 kB: +0.10%
size for +6.7% speed (290058198 at 800.9 vs 289766550 at 750.6), same
as at 4 MB (+0.16% for +6.6%): the stride holds at both block sizes.

Level spacing at 32 kB: 1->2 -8.5% size for -14% speed, 2->3 -2.0% for
-7.8%, 3->4 -0.1% for -6.3%.
